Z||000000||gabriel||02-09-2001||03:19 PM||||Okay, prepping for paint.<P>  What's the best way to remove the exterior trim that runs along the sides?<BR> And the luggage rails on the lid?<P>BTW, looking at that hump on the lid ...<BR>does anyone else think that just cries out for a scoop?  To possibly lower that engine compartment temp? Hmmm ... Starts into fiberglass dreams ...||||reg||1
Z||000001||Raydar||02-09-2001||06:19 PM||||Several members have installed early 80's Mustang scoops backwards on their decklids. Look like they belong there. <BR>Tom (member) used to sell them. Don't have his e-mail handy, though.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 T-top Formula<BR>88 soon-to-be-V6 coupe||||reg||1
Z||000002||MrPBody||02-09-2001||08:45 PM||||<B>Front fender molding</B> has sheet metal hex nuts or speed nuts inside fender.<P><B>Main door molding</B> pries off except one push-in fastener under door handle.  Expect door molding clips to break.<P><B>Short door molding</B> behind handle has one phillips screw from <I>inside</I> door skin.<P><B>Quarter molding</B> has one push-in fastener in door jamb, and maybe a screw through wheel-well liner; otherwise it pries off.  (More self-destructive clips.)<P><B>Luggage rack</B> has two nuts inside deck lid, and many (10, I think) screws through the rails into rubber well nuts in the deck lid.  Pull the flexible inserts out of luggage rack rails to reach attaching screws.<P><B>Rocker panels</B> have screws through whell-well liners, rivets along bottom edge, rivets into door sill (under plastic covers), and tabs into fender and quarter panels.<P>Not as bad as it sounds, except rocker panel rivets.  Good luck!<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Frank Martin||02-09-2001||11:55 PM||||Fiero Store I think is selling the back deck lid scoop for GT's.  You do not have to gut out the hump - just put nice size round holes in the deck lid.  We have found that to work rather well in the Florida heat||||reg||1
